WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:16.199
Hello, I am Ron Vale.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:16.766
Today I have the great

00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:18.066
pleasure to be speaking with

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:20.299
Jim Rothman and Randy Schekman,

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:20.999
who just returned

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.099
to the United States from Stockholm

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.432
where they received the 2013

00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:27.966
Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine

00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:30.699
along with their colleague Tom Sudhof.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.932
This trio was awarded the prize

00:00:32.933 --> 00:00:36.132
for their seminal work in uncovering

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:38.332
the molecular machinery that is involved

00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:40.266
in membrane trafficking

00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:43.466
and protein or neurotransmitter secretion.

00:00:43.467 --> 00:00:45.266
This is a journey of proteins

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:46.366
through a cell

00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:48.699
that starts with protein insertion

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.299
into the endoplasmic reticulum.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.266
Those proteins are then recognized

00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:55.732
as cargo, packaged,

00:00:55.733 --> 00:00:57.799
and then transported

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:58.766
as small vesicles

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:00.232
to the next way station,

00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:01.799
which is the Golgi apparatus.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.466
And from there

00:01:03.467 --> 00:01:05.566
they are transported to new

00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:06.599
membrane destinations,

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.832
such as the lysosome,

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:09.399
or the plasma membrane.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.832
And this trio of Sudhof, Rothman, and

00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:14.966
Schekman found the

00:01:14.967 --> 00:01:16.099
molecular machinery

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.799
that is involved in each step

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:20.099
of this pathway through the cell.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.032
So thank you for joining us today.

00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:26.432
And I'd like to begin

00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:27.366
with the science.

00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:29.666
And maybe, Randy, I could ask you

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:32.732
if you could describe the work

00:01:32.733 --> 00:01:35.666
that Jim did that led to his

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:37.432
contributions for the Nobel Prize.

00:01:37.433 --> 00:01:38.799
Schekman: I am happy to do that.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.366
I first met Jim when he interviewed

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.166
for a faculty position at Berkeley.

00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:45.832
As an assistant professor I had been there

00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:47.766
about a year.

00:01:47.767 --> 00:01:49.332
And he told me in broad

00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:51.199
terms about his plan,

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.699
which I certainly

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.499
appreciated being a biochemist.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.532
And then he went and did it at Stanford.

00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:00.299
Very quickly, he launched

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.599
into developing a cell-free reaction

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.632
to reproduce traffic, at least initially,

00:02:06.633 --> 00:02:07.732
he thought it was traffic

00:02:07.733 --> 00:02:08.899
from the ER to the Golgi.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.466
Subsequently he turned out to be

00:02:11.467 --> 00:02:12.999
measuring traffic,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.766
vesicular traffic, within the Golgi.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:18.032
And he used that to identify

00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:19.632
soluble proteins

00:02:19.633 --> 00:02:22.432
that he called NSF, for N-ethylmaleimide

00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:23.599
sensitive protein

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.332
and its SNAP, which

00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:29.132
was its membrane receptor for NSF,

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:31.666
and showed that these

00:02:31.667 --> 00:02:32.632
were involved at some point

00:02:32.633 --> 00:02:34.566
in membrane fusion.

00:02:34.567 --> 00:02:37.132
And then in a really brilliant experiment,

00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:38.132
several years later,

00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:39.099
he used the

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.666
NSF and alpha-SNAP complex,

00:02:41.667 --> 00:02:45.099
bound by non-hydrolyzable ATP analog,

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.632
to fish out of membranes

00:02:47.633 --> 00:02:50.232
from a crude detergent soluble fraction

00:02:50.233 --> 00:02:51.932
from bovine brain

00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:54.166
the proteins that turned out

00:02:54.167 --> 00:02:56.999
to be the membrane anchors

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.199
and the catalysts for fusion,

00:02:59.200 --> 00:02:59.866
which he called SNARE proteins.

00:02:59.867 --> 00:03:02.366
So it was a really beautiful synthesis

00:03:02.367 --> 00:03:03.932
of the biochemistry

00:03:03.933 --> 00:03:06.732
and functional analysis.

00:03:06.733 --> 00:03:07.932
Vale: And Jim maybe

00:03:07.933 --> 00:03:09.832
I could ask you the same question.

00:03:09.833 --> 00:03:10.666
Could you comment

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:13.066
on the key discoveries and contributions

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:14.099
that Randy made

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.399
that led to his

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.032
being awarded the Nobel Prize?

00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:19.199
Rothman: This is a great pleasure

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.266
to have a chance to.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:22.232
Randy and I have been joking

00:03:22.233 --> 00:03:22.766
for a long time

00:03:22.767 --> 00:03:24.299
about giving each other's seminars,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:25.232
so you've given us

00:03:25.233 --> 00:03:25.999
a great chance to do that, Ron.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:33.032
Randy in the... starting in the late 1970s,

00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:35.199
took on a problem

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.532
that was extremely adventurous.

00:03:38.533 --> 00:03:39.566
And people at that time,

00:03:39.567 --> 00:03:41.599
model organisms were not

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:42.532
what they are today.

00:03:42.533 --> 00:03:44.999
There were very few examples

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.232
where for example yeast had been used

00:03:47.233 --> 00:03:51.199
to really forge ahead and understand

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.299
complex eukaryotic pathways.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.766
As Randy points out,

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.599
the yeast cell cycle was one.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:58.899
People doubted actually

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.599
that there was a secretory pathway in yeast

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:06.699
that reflected in any meaningful way

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.766
the similarity to the Palade pathway

00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:12.066
that we all had understood at the time.

00:04:12.067 --> 00:04:15.199
The Golgi apparatus, if it was observed,

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.532
it was only scantily observed.

00:04:17.533 --> 00:04:20.732
And Randy especially

00:04:20.733 --> 00:04:22.632
in a really innovative approach

00:04:22.633 --> 00:04:24.866
was able to isolate a series of mutations.

00:04:24.867 --> 00:04:28.766
First based on kind of brute screening,

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:30.599
and then after that based on a

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.866
really important experimental insight

00:04:33.867 --> 00:04:35.432
about if you have a cell

00:04:35.433 --> 00:04:36.732
that is making protein

00:04:36.733 --> 00:04:37.632
but not excreting the protein

00:04:37.633 --> 00:04:39.832
it's going to get denser.

00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:40.866
So based on that

00:04:40.867 --> 00:04:42.599
Randy was able to isolate

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:43.999
a large number of mutations

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.866
systematically that essentially defined

00:04:45.867 --> 00:04:46.999
the entire secretory pathway

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.032
and the major secretory organelles in yeast.

00:04:50.033 --> 00:04:52.299
And established the generality

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:53.566
of the pathway. But more importantly

00:04:53.567 --> 00:04:56.032
they provided the means to discover

00:04:56.033 --> 00:04:57.666
the molecular machinery

00:04:57.667 --> 00:05:01.266
that is important in various stages

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:02.499
of vesicle budding and fusion.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:03.299
And it's turned out

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:05.299
that over the years that

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:06.532
the genes that Randy

00:05:06.533 --> 00:05:07.599
initially discovered,

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:08.132
23 of them,

00:05:08.133 --> 00:05:11.566
play pivotal roles in multiple stages

00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:12.332
of the secretory pathway.

00:05:12.333 --> 00:05:15.432
Some at all stages of vesicular trafficking.

00:05:15.433 --> 00:05:18.999
His students and others

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.699
have followed in his footsteps

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:21.299
and have essentially

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.999
laid out the entire map

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.199
of the transport pathways

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:27.032
in the yeast cell.

00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:28.599
Vale: So both of you

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.566
shared some interesting similarities

00:05:30.567 --> 00:05:33.599
in terms of how your careers started.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.266
You're both trained as

00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:38.132
biochemists in some great laboratories.

00:05:38.133 --> 00:05:42.432
And you then became assistant professors

00:05:42.433 --> 00:05:44.132
both at a very young age.

00:05:44.133 --> 00:05:47.432
And then both of you picked

00:05:47.433 --> 00:05:50.099
very risky problems to start your laboratories.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.099
So I'd like to get some background

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.366
of how you came up

00:05:53.367 --> 00:05:54.132
with this project,

00:05:54.133 --> 00:05:57.166
and how you decided to tackle it.

00:05:57.167 --> 00:05:59.299
So maybe I could start with you Randy?

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:03.066
Maybe you can just tell me how you ended up

00:06:03.067 --> 00:06:04.799
choosing the problem membrane secretion

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.266
and also taking a genetic approach.

00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:09.032
Schekman: Near the end of my graduate career,

00:06:09.033 --> 00:06:11.866
in looking at the literature on membranes,

00:06:11.867 --> 00:06:14.266
largely influenced by Bill Wickner

00:06:14.267 --> 00:06:15.732
who was a postdoc in Arthur's lab

00:06:15.733 --> 00:06:17.999
at the time who also had come from Kennedy's lab

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:24.866
that biological membranes were likely to be

00:06:24.867 --> 00:06:28.032
a subject that one could

00:06:28.033 --> 00:06:30.566
learn a great deal about biosynthesis.

00:06:30.567 --> 00:06:32.299
One could possibly treat

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:33.732
a biological membrane

00:06:33.733 --> 00:06:34.832
as a macromolecule

00:06:34.833 --> 00:06:36.399
and dissect its biogenesis

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:37.999
using the techniques of

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:39.232
biochemistry and genetics

00:06:39.233 --> 00:06:40.866
in a way that hadn't really

00:06:40.867 --> 00:06:42.932
been applied yet.

00:06:42.933 --> 00:06:45.332
So I went to John Singer's lab

00:06:45.333 --> 00:06:47.299
to learn about what he was thinking

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:48.499
about biological membranes

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.799
and I found myself on a project

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.532
that didn't really have a future in my mind.

00:06:54.533 --> 00:06:57.799
It was using a scarce material,

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:58.666
and I found that

00:06:58.667 --> 00:07:00.566
working with mammalian cells,

00:07:00.567 --> 00:07:00.832
at least at that time

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:02.332
in the mid 1970s

00:07:02.333 --> 00:07:06.099
was not as satisfying as working with a microorganism.

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:08.566
So I resolved when I joined

00:07:08.567 --> 00:07:09.699
the faculty at Berkeley

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:11.832
influenced to some extent,

00:07:11.833 --> 00:07:13.499
some large extent

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:14.366
by the success of Lee Hartwell

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:16.099
in his genetic dissection

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.199
of the cell division cycle

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:18.166
that I would then

00:07:18.167 --> 00:07:20.499
launch an independent career using

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.299
some kind of genetic analysis

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:26.232
of membrane biogenesis.

00:07:26.233 --> 00:07:27.399
And Peter Novick

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:30.066
was one of my first graduate students

00:07:30.067 --> 00:07:33.299
and it was my good fortune that he joined my lab.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.732
He was a very thoughtful, very precise,

00:07:35.733 --> 00:07:40.266
hardworking, really brilliant student.

00:07:40.267 --> 00:07:41.766
And so we, the two of us,

00:07:41.767 --> 00:07:42.699
sort of embarked on this

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:44.466
effort to identify

00:07:44.467 --> 00:07:46.866
the genes involved in secretion

00:07:46.867 --> 00:07:47.799
by a genetic approach.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:49.932
It was kind of a trial and error.

00:07:49.933 --> 00:07:51.466
Initially we had some crazy idea

00:07:51.467 --> 00:07:53.599
about the need for a genetic selection

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:54.599
that didn't work.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:55.399
We found a mutant in spite

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.399
of it being a selection that didn't work

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:59.366
we found the first mutant anyways,

00:07:59.367 --> 00:08:02.532
and we realized that it was a complex pathway

00:08:02.533 --> 00:08:03.899
and that probably just looking

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.099
through a random assortment

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:06.632
of temperature sensitive mutants,

00:08:06.633 --> 00:08:07.632
just as Hartwell had done,

00:08:07.633 --> 00:08:09.799
would turn up more genes.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:12.666
And we did find another one,

00:08:12.667 --> 00:08:15.066
but as Jim mentioned earlier,

00:08:15.067 --> 00:08:18.332
we decided to use an enrichment procedure

00:08:18.333 --> 00:08:20.132
and isolated a lot more mutations.

00:08:20.133 --> 00:08:24.932
So it was a good choice obviously.

00:08:24.933 --> 00:08:25.932
It worked out well.

00:08:25.933 --> 00:08:28.699
It's not the traditional way that

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:30.032
one starts a career.

00:08:30.033 --> 00:08:31.566
Usually postdocs are

00:08:31.567 --> 00:08:33.232
a little more conservative

00:08:33.233 --> 00:08:35.599
but I always advise my postdocs

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:36.866
to be more bold

00:08:36.867 --> 00:08:38.332
and to do something new.

00:08:38.333 --> 00:08:40.466
But of course when you do something

00:08:40.467 --> 00:08:42.066
new there's a good chance you will fail.

00:08:42.067 --> 00:08:43.899
So what I did was I started

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:45.066
several different projects.

00:08:45.067 --> 00:08:46.966
This one worked within a year.

00:08:46.967 --> 00:08:47.932
And then I marshaled

00:08:47.933 --> 00:08:49.299
all of my efforts to focus on that.

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:51.766
Vale: So as a follow up, I was wondering

00:08:51.767 --> 00:08:54.432
if you could explain the assay that you used

00:08:54.433 --> 00:08:56.932
to get the second mutant.

00:08:56.933 --> 00:08:58.132
And also your reaction

00:08:58.133 --> 00:09:00.699
to that amazing electron micrograph

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:03.666
in the Figure 6 of your 1979 paper

00:09:03.667 --> 00:09:05.932
that shows this yeast cell

00:09:05.933 --> 00:09:08.799
filled with all of these vesicles

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:10.599
that could no longer reach the cell surface.

00:09:10.600 --> 00:09:14.132
Schekman: So the idea was that in looking at

00:09:14.133 --> 00:09:15.932
thin sections in yeast cells

00:09:15.933 --> 00:09:18.832
one can see some organelles

00:09:18.833 --> 00:09:20.732
that look like they could be associated

00:09:20.733 --> 00:09:21.432
with secretion.

00:09:21.433 --> 00:09:22.999
Not as dramatic as in a section

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.932
of mammalian cells, but Novick and I guessed

00:09:25.933 --> 00:09:27.699
that the vesicles that one sees

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:29.966
congregating under the bud portion of a dividing cell

00:09:29.967 --> 00:09:33.232
would be the vehicle not only for secretion,

00:09:33.233 --> 00:09:34.266
but for assembly of the plasma membrane.

00:09:34.267 --> 00:09:35.599
And so we predicted

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.366
that anything that blocked

00:09:37.367 --> 00:09:38.699
the biogenesis of these vesicles

00:09:38.700 --> 00:09:39.832
would be lethal

00:09:39.833 --> 00:09:41.666
and that therefore that one would have

00:09:41.667 --> 00:09:42.732
to go after conditional,

00:09:42.733 --> 00:09:43.799
temperature sensitive

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:44.799
lethal mutations.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:49.132
So that was our first and crucial assumption

00:09:49.133 --> 00:09:51.366
that it was going to be an essential gene.

00:09:51.367 --> 00:09:53.066
And the second was that

00:09:53.067 --> 00:09:54.532
the mutant would,

00:09:54.533 --> 00:09:56.299
at a restrictive temperature,

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:57.599
cause secretory proteins,

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.432
which are otherwise very efficiently

00:09:59.433 --> 00:10:00.666
secreted from the cell,

00:10:00.667 --> 00:10:04.266
to accumulate inside the cell.

00:10:04.267 --> 00:10:06.599
And that actually was a really trivial assay.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.366
You just assay intact cells,

00:10:08.367 --> 00:10:09.999
which measures the enzyme

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.466
that has been trapped in the cell walls.

00:10:11.467 --> 00:10:12.932
The cell wall is porous.

00:10:12.933 --> 00:10:14.232
And then you dissolve the cell wall

00:10:14.233 --> 00:10:15.832
with an enzyme,

00:10:15.833 --> 00:10:17.566
and you have spheroblasts left.

00:10:17.567 --> 00:10:18.532
And then you just rupture

00:10:18.533 --> 00:10:19.932
the spheroblasts with detergent

00:10:19.933 --> 00:10:20.699
and you assay what is

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:23.032
inside the spheroblast,

00:10:23.033 --> 00:10:23.599
and that is a measure of

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:24.366
the accumulation.

00:10:24.367 --> 00:10:25.432
In a wildtype cell,

00:10:25.433 --> 00:10:28.332
that baseline is virtually zero.

00:10:28.333 --> 00:10:30.832
In a sec mutant it rises dramatically

00:10:30.833 --> 00:10:32.299
above the background.

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:32.999
So the first mutant,

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:34.832
just based on a biochemical

00:10:34.833 --> 00:10:37.799
enzyme assay was really obviously

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:39.099
blocked in multiple

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:41.399
secretory enzyme transport.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.032
But the dramatic result came

00:10:43.033 --> 00:10:44.166
just around the time

00:10:44.167 --> 00:10:46.299
that George Palade visited Berkeley

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:47.766
for a special lecture

00:10:47.767 --> 00:10:49.766
and I recall Novick telling me

00:10:49.767 --> 00:10:51.032
at the luncheon that

00:10:51.033 --> 00:10:52.132
Palade had encouraged him

00:10:52.133 --> 00:10:53.399
with these preliminary results

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:54.299
to cut a thin section

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:55.899
and look and see what these cells looked like.

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:59.299
And so we got an expert technical assistant

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:00.166
in the department,

00:11:00.167 --> 00:11:01.966
and that of course was one of the

00:11:01.967 --> 00:11:03.732
great eureka moments of my career

00:11:03.733 --> 00:11:05.532
when Novick excitedly

00:11:05.533 --> 00:11:09.399
called me down to the EM room

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:09.566
and showed me these

00:11:09.567 --> 00:11:11.099
sec1 cell sections

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:13.699
that were like they had measles.

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:14.699
They were just full of vesicles

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.532
and nothing like that

00:11:16.533 --> 00:11:18.399
had ever been published.

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:19.199
I don't think it had ever been

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:20.532
seen before.

00:11:20.533 --> 00:11:21.366
And from that moment,

00:11:21.367 --> 00:11:23.032
literally from that moment

00:11:23.033 --> 00:11:25.299
on, I knew we would have at least

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:28.632
several decades worth of productive work.

00:11:28.633 --> 00:11:30.332
Vale: Well Jim, let me ask you

00:11:30.333 --> 00:11:33.166
a similar question.

00:11:33.167 --> 00:11:34.399
You started off your career at Stanford

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:37.432
as a very young assistant professor

00:11:37.433 --> 00:11:42.132
You had experience with the VSV virus from

00:11:42.133 --> 00:11:43.866
your previous work,

00:11:43.867 --> 00:11:46.066
but you decided to tackle

00:11:46.067 --> 00:11:48.366
a very difficult problem,

00:11:48.367 --> 00:11:49.632
which was to try to reconstitute

00:11:49.633 --> 00:11:53.332
parts of the protein secretion

00:11:53.333 --> 00:11:56.932
pathway in a cell free extract.

00:11:56.933 --> 00:11:59.166
And in fact many people

00:11:59.167 --> 00:12:00.166
thought that wouldn't work.

00:12:00.167 --> 00:12:01.132
And that it would be

00:12:01.133 --> 00:12:02.799
an extremely risky project to take

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:04.066
on so I was wondering if you

00:12:04.067 --> 00:12:06.699
could give a little bit about

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:08.699
your thinking

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:08.866
and the background that

00:12:08.867 --> 00:12:10.899
led to that particular project.

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:13.166
Rothman: My exposure to

00:12:13.167 --> 00:12:15.166
this entire area of

00:12:15.167 --> 00:12:16.332
biology began as a medical

00:12:16.333 --> 00:12:17.732
student in my first year

00:12:17.733 --> 00:12:19.032
at Harvard Medical School.

00:12:19.033 --> 00:12:21.566
And George Palade's

00:12:21.567 --> 00:12:24.599
discoveries were really fresh

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:24.932
and they were presented

00:12:24.933 --> 00:12:27.032
in my histology course.

00:12:27.033 --> 00:12:28.999
And when I saw those

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:30.666
electron micrographs of vesicles

00:12:30.667 --> 00:12:31.799
and endoplasmic reticulum

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:32.299
and Golgi

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:34.466
I immediately started

00:12:34.467 --> 00:12:36.399
thinking given my background

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:39.366
in physical sciences,

00:12:39.367 --> 00:12:40.899
how in the world can such a vesicle from, you know...

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:41.866
what would make that happen,

00:12:41.867 --> 00:12:43.832
and how would it know where to fuse?

00:12:43.833 --> 00:12:46.266
And I must say somehow I identified

00:12:46.267 --> 00:12:47.566
that problem as one that

00:12:47.567 --> 00:12:48.299
I wanted to solve

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.666
at some point and I got fixated on it.

00:12:51.667 --> 00:12:53.166
And I can't really tell you why,

00:12:53.167 --> 00:12:54.499
but I just got fixated on it.

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:57.132
I found it as fascinating then as I do now.

00:12:57.133 --> 00:13:02.232
And after leaving medical school

00:13:02.233 --> 00:13:04.832
and finishing my PhD with Gene Kennedy,

00:13:04.833 --> 00:13:06.632
I did learn some basic biochemistry

00:13:06.633 --> 00:13:08.732
of membranes and lipids.

00:13:08.733 --> 00:13:09.699
I went to work with Harvey Lodish

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:12.266
for a year or two,

00:13:12.267 --> 00:13:13.566
and I did it with the freedom

00:13:13.567 --> 00:13:16.066
of having already my assistant professor

00:13:16.067 --> 00:13:17.599
offer in hand.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:19.099
So I was able to do pretty much what

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:19.732
I wanted and focus

00:13:19.733 --> 00:13:23.099
on really educating myself.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:24.466
And from Harvey and his colleagues

00:13:24.467 --> 00:13:27.132
I learned how to work with

00:13:27.133 --> 00:13:28.232
complex cell free systems

00:13:28.233 --> 00:13:29.699
where we were using cell free extracts

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:32.799
that were synthesizing protein.

00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:34.099
We learned following the footsteps

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:36.899
of Gunter Blobel how to

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:38.766
study the incorporation of those proteins

00:13:38.767 --> 00:13:40.966
in vitro into the endoplasmic reticulum

00:13:40.967 --> 00:13:42.399
membrane and that

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:43.299
really equipped me very well.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:44.432
Now when I went to Stanford

00:13:44.433 --> 00:13:48.366
in 1978, like Randy

00:13:48.367 --> 00:13:50.099
I pursued several different projects.

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:53.532
And that was a way of distributing the risk,

00:13:53.533 --> 00:13:53.799
but then again I made a

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:56.032
decision to focus intensively

00:13:56.033 --> 00:13:57.799
on one project when

00:13:57.800 --> 00:13:59.299
we did have some success

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:00.699
after some period of time,

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:01.532
and that is the project

00:14:01.533 --> 00:14:02.366
you're referring to

00:14:02.367 --> 00:14:04.632
the reconstitution of

00:14:04.633 --> 00:14:05.999
transport in a cell free extract.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:07.132
And I think that decision

00:14:07.133 --> 00:14:08.066
that Randy made

00:14:08.067 --> 00:14:09.166
and the decision that I made

00:14:09.167 --> 00:14:10.432
is a very important

00:14:10.433 --> 00:14:12.032
decision that young scientists

00:14:12.033 --> 00:14:13.166
can make which is

00:14:13.167 --> 00:14:15.399
that at some point you really have to focus.

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:16.899
And you have to focus deeply on something.

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:18.532
And in the long run if you want to make

00:14:18.533 --> 00:14:20.766
a contribution that is really worth making

00:14:20.767 --> 00:14:22.666
rather than simply incremental,

00:14:22.667 --> 00:14:24.199
you have to stick

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:24.966
as Randy did and

00:14:24.967 --> 00:14:26.599
frankly as you did Ron

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:29.332
to that decision.

00:14:29.333 --> 00:14:31.099
It's not always going to be easy.

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:32.432
And it's certainly not free of risk,

00:14:32.433 --> 00:14:34.532
but it has great rewards

00:14:34.533 --> 00:14:36.199
for science when you are successful.

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:37.499
So what we did was we

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:39.966
wanted to reconstitute transport

00:14:39.967 --> 00:14:41.132
from the ER to the Golgi

00:14:41.133 --> 00:14:41.766
so we took the cells,

00:14:41.767 --> 00:14:43.766
living, we gave them a

00:14:43.767 --> 00:14:44.699
very short pulse label

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:46.632
with radioactive methionine,

00:14:46.633 --> 00:14:49.899
and tricks I had learned in Harvey Lodish's lab,

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:51.166
then homogenized

00:14:51.167 --> 00:14:54.432
the cells to retain them,

00:14:54.433 --> 00:14:56.332
the G protein, in the endoplasmic reticulum.

00:14:56.333 --> 00:14:58.366
And then we incubated that extract

00:14:58.367 --> 00:15:00.266
to see if the glycoprotein

00:15:00.267 --> 00:15:01.599
could be further processed

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:05.199
by adding certain sugar residues,

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:05.832
especially N-acetylglucosamine,

00:15:05.833 --> 00:15:06.666
which we knew

00:15:06.667 --> 00:15:08.266
from the work of Phil Robbins

00:15:08.267 --> 00:15:09.399
and Stuart Kornfeld

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:13.132
and others occurred in the Golgi.

00:15:13.133 --> 00:15:14.799
And we could see that processing in vitro,

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:15.699
it would have suggested

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:18.466
the transport occurred.

00:15:18.467 --> 00:15:20.166
And indeed, after I don't know,

00:15:20.167 --> 00:15:21.899
a month or two,

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:22.466
we observed

00:15:22.467 --> 00:15:23.932
that that was the case.

00:15:23.933 --> 00:15:25.032
We could see it.

00:15:25.033 --> 00:15:26.699
But we had a huge background.

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:30.599
Even without any kind of incubation

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:31.966
longer than a few seconds,

00:15:31.967 --> 00:15:34.666
even without adding ATP or cytosol

00:15:34.667 --> 00:15:37.199
we still saw processing and what we

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:38.466
came to realize was that

00:15:38.467 --> 00:15:39.766
we already had too much of the G protein

00:15:39.767 --> 00:15:40.866
sitting in the Golgi

00:15:40.867 --> 00:15:43.099
and it was getting glycosylated

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:45.299
directly there. So that gave me the idea

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.399
of what is now known as a complementation assay

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:49.366
which is pretty much

00:15:49.367 --> 00:15:50.632
the standard design for most

00:15:50.633 --> 00:15:52.499
cell free (in those days)

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:54.966
cell free reconstitution of transport

00:15:54.967 --> 00:15:56.866
and the idea is  you want

00:15:56.867 --> 00:15:57.966
to mix two extracts,

00:15:57.967 --> 00:16:00.632
one from the cell that contains the cargo protein, VSV-G,

00:16:00.633 --> 00:16:02.999
but lacks the processing enzyme,

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:04.466
The other extract

00:16:04.467 --> 00:16:06.432
from the cell that

00:16:06.433 --> 00:16:08.466
lacks the processing enzyme,

00:16:08.467 --> 00:16:09.999
but... excuse me.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:10.966
Has the processing enzyme,

00:16:10.967 --> 00:16:13.299
but lacks the cargo protein.

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:14.999
And so when the two are mixed,

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:16.632
if the cargo protein

00:16:16.633 --> 00:16:18.632
is getting glycosylated,

00:16:18.633 --> 00:16:19.932
it must have gone from

00:16:19.933 --> 00:16:21.099
a donor membrane

00:16:21.100 --> 00:16:24.432
in the extract of the one cell

00:16:24.433 --> 00:16:25.666
to the processing enzyme

00:16:25.667 --> 00:16:26.066
in the Golgi

00:16:26.067 --> 00:16:27.966
of the other cell.

00:16:27.967 --> 00:16:29.566
And so that eliminated the background

00:16:29.567 --> 00:16:30.832
that I referred to

00:16:30.833 --> 00:16:32.632
because the extract

00:16:32.633 --> 00:16:34.332
with the VSV-G protein in it was

00:16:34.333 --> 00:16:37.699
incapable of processing by adding sugar.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:38.932
So when we set that assay

00:16:38.933 --> 00:16:40.466
up it actually worked.

00:16:40.467 --> 00:16:43.266
It was in I believe 1979.

00:16:43.267 --> 00:16:44.332
I still have the gel

00:16:44.333 --> 00:16:45.999
that shows a very, very faint band

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:47.499
of the VSV-G protein being

00:16:47.500 --> 00:16:50.032
processed to what is called an

00:16:50.033 --> 00:16:51.332
endo-H resistant form.

00:16:51.333 --> 00:16:53.666
And it was very, very exciting.

00:16:53.667 --> 00:16:55.732
Vale: Randy, you were

00:16:55.733 --> 00:16:57.599
originally trained as a biochemist

00:16:57.600 --> 00:16:58.399
with one of the master

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:00.066
biochemists, Arthur Kornberg.

00:17:00.067 --> 00:17:02.466
You then began to

00:17:02.467 --> 00:17:03.666
pursue this problem

00:17:03.667 --> 00:17:05.199
of membrane secretion in yeast

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:09.132
using a genetic approach.

00:17:09.133 --> 00:17:10.532
When did you decide it was the right moment

00:17:10.533 --> 00:17:12.266
to start to begin a

00:17:12.267 --> 00:17:13.966
biochemical dissection of this problem?

00:17:13.967 --> 00:17:16.599
Schekman: Our approach to initiate

00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:20.199
with genetics was always, in my mind,

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:25.299
just a stepping-stone into biochemistry.

00:17:25.300 --> 00:17:27.899
It as not going to... I didn't want to pursue it

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.532
purely by genetic analysis.

00:17:30.533 --> 00:17:32.732
I had every intention of using

00:17:32.733 --> 00:17:34.066
this to develop some

00:17:34.067 --> 00:17:35.532
kind of cell-free reaction.

00:17:35.533 --> 00:17:37.799
It was a challenge to get people

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:39.566
in my lab to think that way,

00:17:39.567 --> 00:17:41.499
because when something is working

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:43.032
everybody who joins your lab

00:17:43.033 --> 00:17:44.632
wants to keep doing that thing.

00:17:44.633 --> 00:17:45.866
They wanted to isolate more mutants.

00:17:45.867 --> 00:17:48.132
They wanted to clone more genes,

00:17:48.133 --> 00:17:49.532
but convincing any of them

00:17:49.533 --> 00:17:50.566
to actually take a step back

00:17:50.567 --> 00:17:53.032
and do something very different,

00:17:53.033 --> 00:17:54.732
which to me would have been

00:17:54.733 --> 00:17:55.799
the obvious thing

00:17:55.800 --> 00:17:57.566
was tough.

00:17:57.567 --> 00:18:02.632
So fortunately after six or seven years,

00:18:02.633 --> 00:18:03.832
David Baker joined the lab.

00:18:03.833 --> 00:18:05.532
And he was brash

00:18:05.533 --> 00:18:07.999
and considered our efforts

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:08.832
in that direction

00:18:08.833 --> 00:18:11.432
inefficient and ineffective.

00:18:11.433 --> 00:18:12.666
And within two weeks

00:18:12.667 --> 00:18:15.032
he had developed a cell free reaction

00:18:15.033 --> 00:18:16.599
that reproduced the first half

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:19.799
of the secretory pathway in vitro.

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:20.932
And from that point on,

00:18:20.933 --> 00:18:22.799
everyone that joined the lab said okay,

00:18:22.800 --> 00:18:24.066
I want to do the biochemistry

00:18:24.067 --> 00:18:24.699
because it was obvious

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:26.466
how valuable that would be.

00:18:26.467 --> 00:18:28.766
So what's the lesson here?

00:18:28.767 --> 00:18:29.832
I guess you have to have

00:18:29.833 --> 00:18:31.499
the courage of your own convictions

00:18:31.500 --> 00:18:34.232
and be persistent

00:18:34.233 --> 00:18:34.699
and I think particularly this kind of

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:37.566
biochemical approach if you had

00:18:37.567 --> 00:18:38.666
success in that in the past

00:18:38.667 --> 00:18:40.499
you have as an article of faith,

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:43.099
really certainty,

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:43.999
that you can do it again.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:46.732
And I felt confident that we could

00:18:46.733 --> 00:18:48.332
and of course, Jim had succeeded,

00:18:48.333 --> 00:18:49.399
so I felt doubly confident

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:50.066
that we could do it.

00:18:50.067 --> 00:18:53.232
And that was a plan

00:18:53.233 --> 00:18:54.399
right from the start.

00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:57.232
Vale: Jim, I know you were originally trained

00:18:57.233 --> 00:18:57.932
as a physicist,

00:18:57.933 --> 00:19:00.632
then you did beautiful

00:19:00.633 --> 00:19:03.232
physical chemistry work,

00:19:03.233 --> 00:19:05.599
with Eugene Kennedy,

00:19:05.600 --> 00:19:07.532
and eventually

00:19:07.533 --> 00:19:09.232
you managed to mature

00:19:09.233 --> 00:19:10.199
your research problem

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:11.732
in vesicular trafficking

00:19:11.733 --> 00:19:15.799
to the point where you were able to get

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:19.032
purified membranes and purified proteins

00:19:19.033 --> 00:19:21.966
SNARE proteins, and reconstitute,

00:19:21.967 --> 00:19:24.899
a membrane fusion reaction.

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.066
So that must have been a very satisfying

00:19:27.067 --> 00:19:29.066
moment and I was wondering if you could explain

00:19:29.067 --> 00:19:32.266
how you got to that particular

00:19:32.267 --> 00:19:35.599
transition where you could study

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:38.266
this problem in this really well-defined manner.

00:19:38.267 --> 00:19:40.666
The history of that work

00:19:40.667 --> 00:19:43.932
was we were left logically with NSF, SNAP,

00:19:43.933 --> 00:19:46.166
and SNAREs as components of the fusion

00:19:46.167 --> 00:19:47.032
machinery.

00:19:47.033 --> 00:19:49.766
Bill Wickner did an experiment

00:19:49.767 --> 00:19:51.332
in his vacuole fusion system

00:19:51.333 --> 00:19:55.632
that established actually that NSF

00:19:55.633 --> 00:19:58.499
doesn't participate directly in...

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:00.666
NSF and SNAPs, do not directly participate

00:20:00.667 --> 00:20:02.266
in the fusion reaction per se.

00:20:02.267 --> 00:20:05.299
When put together with our earlier finding

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:07.199
that the ATP hydrolysis by NSF

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:08.466
disrupts the SNARE complex,

00:20:08.467 --> 00:20:11.699
he essentially educated us,

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:12.899
somewhat reluctantly I might add

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:18.732
that NSF must be... while it is critical

00:20:18.733 --> 00:20:19.666
to energize the system

00:20:19.667 --> 00:20:22.332
is acting either after the fusion reaction

00:20:22.333 --> 00:20:25.199
or prior to separating

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:26.366
pre-existing SNARE complexes.

00:20:26.367 --> 00:20:27.632
In any case it left the SNAREs

00:20:27.633 --> 00:20:29.832
pardon me, as the only

00:20:29.833 --> 00:20:31.532
logical candidates. So with that in mind,

00:20:31.533 --> 00:20:34.332
I would say we weren't surprised

00:20:34.333 --> 00:20:35.866
that the SNARE complex

00:20:35.867 --> 00:20:38.032
mediated fusion. And later,

00:20:38.033 --> 00:20:40.166
that year Reinhard Jahn

00:20:40.167 --> 00:20:43.266
and Axel Brunger had published

00:20:43.267 --> 00:20:44.366
the X-ray structure

00:20:44.367 --> 00:20:45.866
that showed a hairpin arrangement

00:20:45.867 --> 00:20:47.432
and so it all really came together

00:20:47.433 --> 00:20:50.199
in that respect in 1998.

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:51.799
And you might say that since then

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:52.399
we've been trying

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:54.632
to study more or less the physical chemistry.

00:20:54.633 --> 00:20:57.999
My current focus is

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:01.232
the still unresolved problem of exactly how

00:21:01.233 --> 00:21:03.332
calcium, synaptotagmin,

00:21:03.333 --> 00:21:04.199
and complexin,

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:05.232
put a brake on fusion

00:21:05.233 --> 00:21:06.332
at the last millisecond

00:21:06.333 --> 00:21:07.432
in the case of the synapse.

00:21:07.433 --> 00:21:09.632
And how they orchestrate

00:21:09.633 --> 00:21:10.932
in a highly

00:21:10.933 --> 00:21:12.032
amplified and synchronous fashion

00:21:12.033 --> 00:21:14.132
the release of the neurotransmitters.

00:21:14.133 --> 00:21:16.499
That also is a problem in physical biochemistry,

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:19.932
which I find enormously satisfying.

00:21:19.933 --> 00:21:21.566
So it's sort of fun for me

00:21:21.567 --> 00:21:24.299
towards the end of a

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:26.032
relatively long career,

00:21:26.033 --> 00:21:27.266
it’s been forty years almost

00:21:27.267 --> 00:21:33.032
in research since I left university

00:21:33.033 --> 00:21:34.566
to come back

00:21:34.567 --> 00:21:37.799
and have an opportunity

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:38.899
to really take this problem

00:21:38.900 --> 00:21:40.399
to the structural and biochemical level

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:42.899
Vale: So I am curious

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:44.532
when you first became

00:21:44.533 --> 00:21:45.999
aware of each other's work

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:48.432
and also while all these

00:21:48.433 --> 00:21:50.166
exciting developments were happening

00:21:50.167 --> 00:21:51.699
in each of your own labs,

00:21:51.700 --> 00:21:52.999
were the two of you in communication

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:53.866
with one another?

00:21:53.867 --> 00:21:55.932
Schekman: We were in touch with each other.

00:21:55.933 --> 00:21:56.532
I mean after all,

00:21:56.533 --> 00:21:57.366
Jim was at Stanford

00:21:57.367 --> 00:21:58.299
and I was at Berkeley.

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:00.966
We actually had one or two

00:22:00.967 --> 00:22:02.499
day long joint group meetings

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:03.166
Rothman: Two.

00:22:03.167 --> 00:22:04.199
Schekman: Was it two?

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:06.732
There was some tension

00:22:06.733 --> 00:22:07.966
between us at the time.

00:22:07.967 --> 00:22:08.932
I think it was because

00:22:08.933 --> 00:22:10.932
you know, we both wanted to make our name

00:22:10.933 --> 00:22:13.299
and you know,

00:22:13.300 --> 00:22:14.399
it was a new area,

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:15.199
and we were carving out

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:19.399
our own approach, and that was...

00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:20.232
Rothman: But we still were very

00:22:20.233 --> 00:22:20.932
much in communication.

00:22:20.933 --> 00:22:23.332
It was very open.

00:22:23.333 --> 00:22:24.732
And I'll tell you something that's a little

00:22:24.733 --> 00:22:26.099
different today.

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:28.832
Oftentimes, there were never times,

00:22:28.833 --> 00:22:30.399
when you didn't share an idea with me

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:31.732
that you were thinking about.

00:22:31.733 --> 00:22:33.566
We weren't doing exactly the same experiments.

00:22:33.567 --> 00:22:36.366
And I would always feel comfortable talking to you

00:22:36.367 --> 00:22:38.299
about something that I was thinking about,

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:39.199
even before we started the experiment.

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:42.399
Because, you know, there's just

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:44.599
a certain pride of craftsmanship

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:47.099
and respect that really good colleagues

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:47.732
have even if they are

00:22:47.733 --> 00:22:48.899
competitive with each other.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:51.766
I think we always had that.

00:22:51.767 --> 00:22:53.732
But there was still some tension.

00:22:53.733 --> 00:22:57.699
And I think that dissolved

00:22:57.700 --> 00:22:59.432
later on, especially,

00:22:59.433 --> 00:23:02.132
when it became clear that we

00:23:02.133 --> 00:23:03.332
had identified genes that

00:23:03.333 --> 00:23:05.066
were coding proteins

00:23:05.067 --> 00:23:06.166
that we could study.

00:23:06.167 --> 00:23:08.566
At that point, it actually...

00:23:08.567 --> 00:23:11.466
we both felt that we could establish ourselves

00:23:11.467 --> 00:23:14.066
at the same time, but

00:23:14.067 --> 00:23:15.799
through a different approach.

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:17.832
Vale: So obviously both of you

00:23:17.833 --> 00:23:21.266
have had incredibly exciting careers in the field of

00:23:21.267 --> 00:23:23.466
membrane trafficking.

00:23:23.467 --> 00:23:25.766
If you had a chance to do it all over again,

00:23:25.767 --> 00:23:29.432
right now, would this be the scientific

00:23:29.433 --> 00:23:32.099
problem that you would pursue?

00:23:32.100 --> 00:23:33.332
Schekman: Jim and I have had this conversation,

00:23:33.333 --> 00:23:36.966
which is if we were young,

00:23:36.967 --> 00:23:39.766
again would we start in this field

00:23:39.767 --> 00:23:44.166
all over again? And you know, in 2013.

00:23:44.167 --> 00:23:46.199
And the answer for very personal reasons,

00:23:46.200 --> 00:23:48.366
is no. I would not.

00:23:48.367 --> 00:23:50.466
And I think Jim shares this feeling.

00:23:50.467 --> 00:23:51.266
Jim: Yeah, I wouldn't either.

00:23:51.267 --> 00:23:51.932
Schekman: Because you know,

00:23:51.933 --> 00:23:54.166
it's a relatively mature field.

00:23:54.167 --> 00:23:55.632
I think there are many interesting things to discover.

00:23:55.633 --> 00:23:58.632
But, you know,

00:23:58.633 --> 00:24:00.332
you heard what I said earlier.

00:24:00.333 --> 00:24:01.832
I wanted to make my own identity.

00:24:01.833 --> 00:24:02.599
I didn't want to be

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:04.132
in someone else's shadow.

00:24:04.133 --> 00:24:05.766
And so for that reason,

00:24:05.767 --> 00:24:07.632
if I were starting over again

00:24:07.633 --> 00:24:10.066
I would pick something brand new

00:24:10.067 --> 00:24:13.566
that was underexplored, and where

00:24:13.567 --> 00:24:15.166
I'd have a lot of time

00:24:15.167 --> 00:24:18.232
and freedom to develop

00:24:18.233 --> 00:24:22.399
a novel approach and make new discoveries,

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:24.799
really significant new discoveries.

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:26.666
So I don't mean to discourage

00:24:26.667 --> 00:24:28.466
people from thinking that way,

00:24:28.467 --> 00:24:30.299
Jim: If I could say at another level,

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:32.232
I totally agree with what you said, Randy.

00:24:32.233 --> 00:24:33.766
But it all depends on

00:24:33.767 --> 00:24:34.799
your scientific focus.

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:36.166
So if I was a young...

00:24:36.167 --> 00:24:38.566
I am a professor of chemistry at Yale.

00:24:38.567 --> 00:24:39.699
I am getting a lot of interest

00:24:39.700 --> 00:24:41.632
in my work now from the chemists.

00:24:41.633 --> 00:24:44.999
You know, because at another level,

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:46.332
this is now a problem

00:24:46.333 --> 00:24:48.566
that where the biology is considered

00:24:48.567 --> 00:24:49.399
relatively solved.

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:51.232
Nothing is ever completely solved.

00:24:51.233 --> 00:24:54.299
Nobel Prizes aren't given out for stuff

00:24:54.300 --> 00:24:55.199
that is basically solved.

00:24:55.200 --> 00:24:57.966
On the other hand, it's a problem that has just been defined

00:24:57.967 --> 00:24:59.132
from the point of view

00:24:59.133 --> 00:25:00.666
of the physical biochemistry

00:25:00.667 --> 00:25:01.832
and the chemical biology

00:25:01.833 --> 00:25:02.799
that can come from it.

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:05.199
So depending on your world view,

00:25:05.200 --> 00:25:07.299
for cell biologists or developmental biologists,

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:09.132
there's probably other things to do

00:25:09.133 --> 00:25:10.766
that are really bigger challenges today.

00:25:10.767 --> 00:25:15.366
For chemists, physical chemists, even physicists,

00:25:15.367 --> 00:25:17.566
and people interested in modeling

00:25:17.567 --> 00:25:20.600
it's really, really primary.

